###############################################################################
# smarter-sh/automation environment variables, for working on this repository
# from your own computer.
#
# `make init` copies this file to '.env' the first time you run it. Each
# variable here is the local copy of a repository secret of the same name
# (Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions), except GH_TOKEN, which is the
# PAT secret under the name that gh reads. The workflows never read this file:
# on GitHub, the secrets are the source.
#
# Everything is optional. Set only what the task at hand needs:
#   - reading runs and their logs (gh run list, the automation-ci-failures
#     skill's fetch-run-logs.sh): GH_TOKEN,
#   - trying a script's Slack post: SLACK_BOT_TOKEN,
#   - reproducing a pod log review: the Claude and AWS variables.
#
# Do NOT commit the .env file: .gitignore ignores it. This repository is
# public. A placeholder that begins with SET-ME is unset.
#
# To load it into your shell: set -a; source .env; set +a
###############################################################################

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GH_TOKEN (the PAT secret)
# A GitHub classic personal access token, ghp_..., with the public_repo scope.
# gh reads it, so you don't need a gh login. The workflows use it, as the PAT
# secret, to push branches and open issues and pull requests in the other
# smarter-sh repositories. Locally, use it for reading (gh run list, gh run
# view, gh pr list): never start, re-run or cancel a workflow by hand.
# see: https://github.com/settings/tokens
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GH_TOKEN=SET-ME-PLEASE

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN (OPTIONAL)
# The Smarter Slack app's bot token, xoxb-..., with the scope chat:write, and
# chat:write.public for a channel that the bot is not a member of.
# scripts/slack_post.py reads it, and posts nothing without it. Prefer
# --dry-run, which prints the message instead of posting it.
# see: https://api.slack.com/apps
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=SET-ME-PLEASE

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (OPTIONAL)
# Claude Code's credential, for the jobs that run it (podLogReview.yml).
# scripts/pod_log/review.py uses CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (a Claude
# subscription; create it with `claude setup-token`) when it is set, else
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (API credit). Set one.
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=SET-ME-PLEASE
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=SET-ME-PLEASE

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_REGION and EKS_CLUSTER_NAME
# (OPTIONAL)
# Read access to the EKS cluster that runs the Smarter platform, for the pod
# log review: `aws eks update-kubeconfig`, then kubectl get, describe and logs,
# through smarter's read-only skills/smarter-kubectl/scripts/kubectl.sh.
# AWS_PROFILE may replace the keypair. These credentials reach production:
# never use them to write to the cluster or to AWS.
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=SET-ME-PLEASE
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=SET-ME-PLEASE
# AWS_PROFILE=default
# EKS_CLUSTER_NAME=SET-ME-PLEASE
